  //: Turn an `Image` into the stand-in for a picture that did not arrive.
  //:
  //: Each engine draws a failed <img> its own way — Chromium a broken-image
  //: glyph, WebKit a hollow grey box, Firefox the alt text, painted past
  //: the box over whatever sits beside it (a country's name across the
  //: phone number in a table cell). And each sizes it its own way where
  //: the author gave no size: 71×23, 55×23 and 60×60 for one unsized
  //: Image, and 240×240 in WebKit for one given only `width=240`. A
  //: picture that LOADED has none of that, so the stand-in is one: the
  //: blank above, which no engine can fail to draw.
  //:
  //: The SAME element rather than a replacement — which is how a Dropdown's
  //: option image goes — because an Image is a widget other code has hold
  //: of: `Stack` placed it in its grid cell, `Tooltip(...)`, `title=` and
  //: `on_mouse_enter=` hung listeners on it, `visible=` set its display.
  //: A swapped-in span would carry none of that until the next render,
  //: and on a page that does not re-render, never. Keeping the <img> also
  //: keeps its `alt` exactly where a screen reader looks for it, and a
  //: decorative one (alt="") stays out of the way.
  //:
  //: The box: whatever width and height the author gave, inline, as
  //: before; a dimension they did NOT give is 0 (`.pg-image--missing`),
  //: which is the box every engine gives an Image while it is still
  //: loading. So the failure moves nothing: the layout it leaves is the
  //: layout it had.
