  //: An option's `code` — an ISO code, a currency code, a language tag — as
  //: the text to draw, or null when there is none to draw. An empty string
  //: is none: an empty span would still take a gap on each side of it.
  Workpeg._optionCode = function (opt) {
    if (!opt || typeof opt !== "object" || opt.code == null) return null;
    var code = String(opt.code);
    return code === "" ? null : code;
  };

  //: Everything a person might type to find one row, lower-cased once.
  //: The label and description are what the row shows; the code and the
  //: meta are what a person often KNOWS instead (an ISO code, a dialling
  //: code); `keywords` is the names the list does not show at all.
  //:
  //: NEVER THE VALUE. A value is what the handler receives, and in a real
  //: list it is usually an opaque id — a row's UUID — that nobody sees and
  //: nobody types. Searching it made every hex letter a match: in a picker
  //: of access groups keyed by UUID, "a" found rows whose names have no
  //: "a", and "c0ffee" found one whose name is nothing like it. A value
  //: worth finding by is shown, so it belongs in `code` or `meta`; one that
  //: is not shown belongs in `keywords`. A plain-string option is the
  //: exception only in appearance: its value IS its label (`text`).
